# Trivy ignore list — every entry here is a deliberate, documented exception, not a
# silent suppression. Anything NOT listed here still blocks CI/CD (ci.yml's `security`
# job, cd.yml's image scan), exactly as intended.

# --- mlflow 2.22.5: fixed only in mlflow>=3.x (ADR-0017) ---
# A real, disclosed finding from Phase 14's live verification, not new: upgrading past
# mlflow 2.x is a genuine major-version migration (a different tracking/registry client
# API) that needs its own scoped effort and testing, not a blind dependency bump forced
# through by a CI gate. Tracked as real follow-up work; see ADR-0017's "Live verification"
# section for the full account of how this was found.
CVE-2025-15036
CVE-2025-15379
CVE-2026-0596
CVE-2026-2635
CVE-2026-2651
CVE-2026-4035
CVE-2026-64849
CVE-2025-10279
CVE-2025-14279
CVE-2025-14287
CVE-2025-15031
CVE-2026-2033
CVE-2026-2393
CVE-2026-2614
CVE-2026-2652
CVE-2026-4137
CVE-2026-8147

# --- pyarrow 19.0.1: transitively pinned by mlflow 2.22.5, not a direct dependency ---
# `uv lock --upgrade-package pyarrow` (tried live, alongside the pillow/black/gitpython/
# sqlparse bumps this same pass fixed) left this version untouched — mlflow 2.x's own
# resolver constraints hold it here. Resolves itself once the mlflow migration above
# happens; not independently fixable before then.
CVE-2026-25087

# --- Helm ConfigMap KSV-0109: false positive, not an actual secret ---
# `JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_MINUTES`/`JWT_REFRESH_TOKEN_DAYS` are plain integers (a token lifetime
# in minutes/days) — the check's key-name heuristic flags anything containing "JWT",
# regardless of what the value actually is. The real secret, `JWT_SECRET_KEY`, is already
# correctly in `secret.yaml`, not here.
KSV-0109

# --- Terraform AWS-0104: unrestricted egress on the reference security group ---
# `deploy/terraform/modules/managed-postgres` — outbound-only, to let the instance reach
# package/OS update endpoints; this is deliberately reference infrastructure never
# actually applied in this environment (see `deploy/terraform/README.md`), not a live
# production security group. A real deployment should scope this down before `apply`.
AVD-AWS-0104

# --- Terraform AWS-0132: S3 buckets use SSE-S3 (AES256), not a customer-managed KMS key ---
# `deploy/terraform/modules/object-storage` — same reference-infrastructure caveat as
# AWS-0104 above: SSE-S3 is still real encryption at rest, just not CMK-managed. A real
# deployment wanting key rotation/access-policy control over the KMS key itself should
# add one before `apply`; not invented here without a concrete requirement driving it.
AVD-AWS-0132

# --- Helm KSV-0014: `readOnlyRootFilesystem` not set on any of the 6 chart Deployments ---
# See `_helpers.tpl`'s own comment on `factoryai.podSecurityContext`: every one of these
# images (torch's HF cache, Postgres/Redis/MinIO's own data/temp writes) has real,
# unaudited local write paths. Declaring the root filesystem read-only requires
# identifying and mounting an `emptyDir` for each of them, then live-verifying the pods
# still start — real, tracked follow-up work, not attempted blind here.
KSV-0014

# --- CD image scan: msgpack/setuptools "Installed Version" is stale, not real ---
# `factoryai.Dockerfile` explicitly floors both (msgpack>=1.2.1, setuptools>=78.1.1) in
# the same pip invocation as the main extras install — confirmed in the build log itself:
# "Successfully installed ... msgpack-1.2.1 ... setuptools-84.0.0 ...". Reproduced
# identically across three consecutive live CD runs (one with Trivy's cache enabled, two
# with it explicitly disabled): the vulnerability table claims "Installed Version" 1.1.2 /
# 70.3.0, while that SAME scan's own "Report Summary" a few lines earlier correctly lists
# only `msgpack-1.2.1.dist-info`/`setuptools-84.0.0.dist-info`, each with zero findings —
# a contradiction inside one scan's own output that cache-disabling didn't change,
# pointing at a genuine bug in this Trivy version's python-pkg vulnerability matching, not
# a real vulnerability in this image. Re-verify the "Report Summary" still confirms the
# fixed versions before ever removing these two lines.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273
